WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.708 --> 00:00:08.000
AMEER ABBAS: I work with a
lot of enterprise customers.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.130
And the biggest question
I get from them is--

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:11.240
How does Google do it?

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.830
How does Google build
globally scalable services

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.530
that for the most part stay up?

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:18.613
Things like Search,
YouTube, Maps Gmail.

00:00:18.613 --> 00:00:20.030
So today I thought
what I would do

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:22.458
is I would actually take
you behind the curtain.

00:00:22.458 --> 00:00:24.500
And let's take a peek at
what that platform looks

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.750
like from the eyes
of a service mesh.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:29.403
We've all heard things like
Istio and Anthos Service Mesh.

00:00:29.403 --> 00:00:30.320
But what do they mean?

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:33.560
And how do I build globally
scalable services with that?

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:34.890
My name is Ameer Abbas.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.270
I'm a solutions architect
out of San Francisco.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.230
And I focus on
distributed platforms,

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:41.567
specifically with
service mesh in mind.

00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:43.650
So this is kind of what
we're going to talk about.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.192
We're going to really
talk about what

00:00:45.192 --> 00:00:48.133
does your application actually
need and what kind of choices

00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:49.550
they have when it
comes to putting

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.470
your application on a platform.

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:54.020
We're going to talk about
multiple Kubernetes clusters

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.360
and what does that mean
for distributed services.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.732
So most of us are getting into
the world of containerization.

00:00:59.732 --> 00:01:01.190
And containerization
and Kubernetes

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:02.245
are kind of synonymous.

00:01:02.245 --> 00:01:04.370
So I'm going to take that
one step further and talk

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:06.103
about multiple
Kubernetes clusters.

00:01:06.103 --> 00:01:08.270
Then we're going to take a
step back and really look

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:09.685
at why service meshes exist.

00:01:09.685 --> 00:01:11.810
Then we're going to look
at the actual service mesh

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:14.170
implementation with Istio
and Anthos Service Mesh

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:16.720
and how they actually perform
and what they actually do.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.470
And then we'll bring
all of these concepts

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:21.650
together and build a platform
that can run globally

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:23.790
scalable, resilient services.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:24.937
So let's get started.

00:01:24.937 --> 00:01:26.270
Let's start with an application.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:28.370
Typically, we think of
from a very infrastructure,

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:29.930
platforming point of view.

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:31.698
We think of-- What
does the platform need?

00:01:31.698 --> 00:01:33.740
I want to flip that model,
and let's talk about--

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:35.460
What do applications need?

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.120
And applications have
a lot of choices.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:40.760
Just in GCP, we have these
three main compute choices.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:42.260
On the far left-hand
side, you've

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:45.320
got platform as a
service with App Engine.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.215
This is a very opinionated
system that you use.

00:01:48.215 --> 00:01:49.590
On the other end
of the spectrum,

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:50.450
you've got Compute Engine.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:51.890
This is infrastructure
as a service.

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:53.807
It doesn't give you
anything, but it gives you

00:01:53.807 --> 00:01:54.740
a lot of flexibility.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.990
You have to build the
entire platform yourselves.

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:58.430
Both of these are very extreme.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.680
On one side, you're
very opinionated, can't

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.570
bring in any of the
opinions that you'd like.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:04.933
On the other side, you're
maybe too unopinionated,

00:02:04.933 --> 00:02:06.350
and you have to
do all of the work

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.600
and all of the operations
and management yourself.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.600
But then along comes
Kubernetes, and it gives us

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.680
a really nice middle point.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.600
It gives us that
automation and abstraction

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.420
away from infrastructure.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:20.090
And it gives us that declarative
way of deploying applications.

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:22.967
So, great, let's deploy our
applications on Kubernetes.

00:02:22.967 --> 00:02:24.800
But what we've learned
is that you typically

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.895
don't deploy the applications
right on Kubernetes.

00:02:26.895 --> 00:02:28.250
Kubernetes is a great scheduler.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:30.230
It's a great container
orchestration solution.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:32.000
But it doesn't give
you everything.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.740
You need to still
build a platform

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:37.250
on top of Kubernetes-- things
like observability and security

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:38.210
and software delivery.

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:40.867
That all needs to happen
up top of Kubernetes.

00:02:40.867 --> 00:02:42.950
So all of those opinions
that you bring in kind of

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:45.830
puts you in the middle of
this very opinionated path.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:47.330
And somewhere in
there's Kubernetes.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:48.392
And this is the arrow.

00:02:48.392 --> 00:02:49.850
Where that arrow
sits is what we're

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:52.343
going to talk about
today is where that is.

00:02:52.343 --> 00:02:53.510
Let's talk about Kubernetes.

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:55.780
So most of us have
at least started

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:58.240
to take the journey into
Kubernetes with container

00:02:58.240 --> 00:02:59.810
orchestration and stuff.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:01.640
So why do we need
multiple clusters?

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:03.682
Well, since we're talking
about building globally

00:03:03.682 --> 00:03:05.470
scalable services,
by definition,

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:08.230
we're talking about multiple
locations or multiple regions.

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:10.930
A Kubernetes cluster can only
reside in a single region.

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:12.430
So if you're in two
or more regions,

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:14.892
you're already in
multiple cluster land.

00:03:14.892 --> 00:03:16.850
Now, within a region,
when I talk to customers,

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:18.892
they talk about, "Well,
how do I make my services

00:03:18.892 --> 00:03:20.020
resilient within a region?"

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:22.090
One way to do that is to
have multiple clusters

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:23.050
within a region.

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:24.685
Two is better than one.

00:03:24.685 --> 00:03:26.920
One goes down, you
have something else.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.350
So we just focus on
those two aspects--

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:32.500
multiregionality, clusters
in different regions,

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.810
and regional resiliency,
multiple clusters

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:36.970
in a single region
that gives you that.

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:39.100
And this spawns this
idea of something

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:40.662
called a distributed service.

00:03:40.662 --> 00:03:42.370
The challenge with
the Kubernetes service

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:43.990
is that Kubernetes
service's fate

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:47.060
is tethered to the fate of
the cluster that it runs on.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.790
Now, a service is a
very important thing.

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:50.110
That's your revenue generator.

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:51.430
That's what makes you money.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.530
A cluster is just a
piece of infrastructure

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:55.240
that just runs your platform.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:56.688
It actually costs you money.

00:03:56.688 --> 00:03:58.480
So tying those two, or
tethering those two,

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.580
together doesn't make sense.

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:04.390
Distributed service, at a very
simplistic definition, is--

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:06.850
How can I put the same
service on multiple Kubernetes

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:07.430
clusters?

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:08.972
That's what a
distributed service is.

00:04:08.972 --> 00:04:10.520
It's a very, very
simple concept.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.240
What it does is it abstracts
your service's lifecycle

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:14.490
from your cluster lifecycle.

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:16.750
If I'm running on
multiple clusters,

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:18.820
I can have clusters
going up and down,

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.920
and my services can remain up.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:22.760
Visually, it would look
something like this.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:24.545
So here, I'm
representing two regions.

00:04:24.545 --> 00:04:26.170
On the left-hand
side, I have region 1.

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:28.220
On the right-hand
side, I have region 2.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:30.560
And then we have multiple
clusters in each region.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:33.790
So on the left-hand side,
we have two zonal clusters--

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:36.440
one in each zone A and zone
B. And on the right-hand side,

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:37.772
I have two zonal clusters.

00:04:37.772 --> 00:04:39.730
And I have a single
distributed service running

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:41.350
on all these four clusters.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:43.120
So not only do I
have this service

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:46.600
that spans multiple regions, but
it also spans multiple clusters

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:47.650
within a region.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:51.310
So I'm getting scalability,
and I'm getting resiliency.

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:53.470
I want to plant that
idea because it opens up

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:56.620
a lot of extra knobs when you
think in terms of distributed

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:58.270
services.

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:00.970
Things like I have to look
at service level objectives

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:04.600
at a service layer, not at
individual workload running

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:05.870
on each of these clusters.

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:08.020
If I can provide my
users with the SLO

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:09.580
that I've promised
them, it doesn't

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:11.038
matter whether it's
running on four

00:05:11.038 --> 00:05:12.760
clusters or a single cluster.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:14.660
Capacity planning
becomes easier.

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:17.630
We've heard terms like
N plus 1 and N plus 2.

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:20.650
If N is the number of clusters
that I need to run and satisfy

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:23.770
the requirements for capacity
for a particular service,

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:25.300
then N plus 1 and N plus 2 is--

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:28.520
Can I withstand a single
failure or double failure?

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:31.270
So we typically think in terms
of N plus 1 and N plus 2.

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:33.500
You can't do that
with a single cluster.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:36.658
Rollouts and rollbacks can be
done at a very granular level.

00:05:36.658 --> 00:05:38.950
So instead of pushing out a
new version of your service

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:41.113
to a single cluster and
to every single cluster,

00:05:41.113 --> 00:05:43.780
I may want to just push out that
new version to a single cluster

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:47.020
in one zone and just have a
subset of my users use it,

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:49.660
test it out, and
then roll it out

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:51.483
to every single other cluster.

00:05:51.483 --> 00:05:52.900
Internally at
Google, we use terms

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:54.160
like draining and spilling.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:56.890
And it sounds exactly
like what it means.

00:05:56.890 --> 00:05:58.750
If a cluster is having
an issue, or if I

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:01.450
want to upgrade a cluster,
I can drain traffic away

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:04.030
from that workload running
on that particular cluster

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.192
and spill it to other clusters.

00:06:06.192 --> 00:06:07.900
So I still haven't
taken my service down,

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:09.820
but I've now freed
up that cluster.

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:11.320
And I can do
whatever maintenance

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:14.123
or operations or upgrades
that I want on that cluster.

00:06:14.123 --> 00:06:16.540
So you can see that when you
think in terms of distributed

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:19.660
services, a lot of these
resiliency and scalability

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:21.530
knobs just kind
of open up to you.

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:23.005
So I want to park that idea.

00:06:23.005 --> 00:06:24.880
And let's switch to why
service meshes exist.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:27.380
And then we'll bring
these two ideas together.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:29.710
So for this, I'm going
to go back in time

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:32.620
and look at this
monolithic application

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:34.208
that we've heard so much about.

00:06:34.208 --> 00:06:35.750
And what is a
monolithic application?

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:37.250
It's just a collection
of functions.

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:39.208
But the first thing is
a monolithic application

00:06:39.208 --> 00:06:40.220
is not a bad thing.

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:42.230
It's actually a
very deterministic--

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:43.240
It's a very good thing.

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:45.970
When function A calls
function B, it always works.

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:48.460
And it always works in a
very deterministic way.

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:49.428
It's a function call.

00:06:49.428 --> 00:06:51.220
I don't have to worry
about is the function

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:52.360
B going to respond or not.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:53.410
It is going to respond.

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:55.390
And if it doesn't, I can
just look at the logs,

00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:57.223
and it's usually the
problem is in the code.

00:06:57.223 --> 00:06:59.373
And I can just go
ahead and fix that.

00:06:59.373 --> 00:07:01.040
Then someone smart
comes along and says,

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:02.240
"We're going to
containerize everything."

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:03.500
And all that means
is I'm going to take

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:04.958
those individual
functions, and I'm

00:07:04.958 --> 00:07:07.968
going to run them as
individual quantums of compute.

00:07:07.968 --> 00:07:09.260
And that's what a container is.

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:12.010
It's just functions
running in containers.

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:13.760
So the first thing
we've done is introduce

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:14.960
this concept of a network.

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:17.660
Now, this is the crux of
why service meshes exist.

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:19.790
This is the bane
of the problem--

00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:21.720
is that we've taken
a function call,

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:23.980
and we've converted that
into a network call.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:25.670
So the first thing we need
is we need a different way

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:27.320
to connect these
two things together.

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:30.860
We need an interface
or an API for example.

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:32.840
Most languages come
with their own APIs.

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:36.470
Or you can use an open
source API library like GRPC

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:37.467
and stick that on.

00:07:37.467 --> 00:07:38.300
And now you're good.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:39.290
You've got your client stubs.

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:40.520
You've got your server stubs.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:42.140
These things can
talk to each other.

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:42.560
Well, that's great.

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:44.900
We've kind of solved just
the connectivity problem.

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:46.250
What about security?

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:48.550
We've heard these terms
zero trust network.

00:07:48.550 --> 00:07:50.660
At a very simplistic
term, all that means

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:54.090
is I'm not going to trust
any form of a network,

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:56.510
whether it's an
external internet

00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:58.940
or whether it's an
internal intranet.

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:01.160
So, in traditional
terms, what we typically

00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:04.760
do is we really fortify the
periphery of our networks

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:07.710
with very solid,
big iron firewalls.

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:09.720
And we make them
very, very strong.

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:11.665
So it's very hard to
penetrate those walls.

00:08:11.665 --> 00:08:13.040
But as soon as
you're in, we just

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:16.208
implicitly trust
everything on our intranet.

00:08:16.208 --> 00:08:18.500
As you get into the world of
containers and distributed

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:21.620
systems, we realize that
even within the network

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:23.520
we need an extra
layer of security.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:25.100
And this is the
concept of zero trust

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:27.860
network, where it's we're not
going to trust any network.

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:29.780
Each container and
each service must

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:31.965
be able to identify itself.

00:08:31.965 --> 00:08:33.840
And this ID becomes a
very important concept.

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:35.215
So that's the
first thing we need

00:08:35.215 --> 00:08:37.460
is now every service must
have an identification,

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:40.850
must have some way to identify
itself to other services.

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:42.522
Now, if I'm a
developer, and let's say

00:08:42.522 --> 00:08:44.480
I'm just starting into
the world of containers,

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:48.560
I would go in Google, Python,
or Go security libraries,

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:50.980
and I would get these
libraries that do this for me.

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:53.940
And I would just implement those
libraries as part of my code.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:55.940
And now what I've gotten
is I have gotten things

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:56.840
like authentication.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:59.720
I can authenticate myself to
other services-- maybe even

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:00.710
authorization.

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:03.260
Am I allowed to do certain
actions to other services?

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:04.862
And I can get things
like encryption.

00:09:04.862 --> 00:09:06.320
Because we don't
trust the network,

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:08.737
we don't want to have open
communication between these two

00:09:08.737 --> 00:09:09.260
things.

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:10.427
Now everything is encrypted.

00:09:10.427 --> 00:09:14.940
It doesn't have to happen over
a VPN or over the same network.

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:16.640
The next thing we
need is this idea

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:19.040
that networks are not resilient.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:20.870
Networks are very unreliable.

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:22.490
When networks go
down, what happens?

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:24.200
When container A
calls container B,

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:26.880
and container B doesn't
respond back immediately,

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:27.570
what do you do?

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:28.370
Do you retry?

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:29.660
How often do you retry?

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:30.470
When do you stop?

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:34.070
You can build that logic
inside of your code,

00:09:34.070 --> 00:09:36.140
or you can get a network
resilience library.

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:38.090
Again, as a developer,
I would go on Google.

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:39.713
I would Google something.

00:09:39.713 --> 00:09:40.630
I would get a library.

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:42.130
I would slap that
onto my container.

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:44.660
And great, now I've taken
care of these network

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:48.530
idiosyncrasies-- latency, fault
tolerance, things going down.

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:50.588
We'll talk about
circuit breaker shortly.

00:09:50.588 --> 00:09:52.130
Since everything is
now in a network,

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:55.580
and networks are not
infinite resources,

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:58.830
we need to figure out how to
put network policies on things.

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:00.758
How often container
A call container B?

00:10:00.758 --> 00:10:02.300
We don't want to
bog down container B

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:04.382
if container A is
just hammering this.

00:10:04.382 --> 00:10:06.590
So we need to be able to
put some sort of rate limits

00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:10.190
and quotas on a per service
level and maybe even granularly

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:11.820
on individual services.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:14.520
So now I need some sort
of a network policy.

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:16.550
And then last but not
least is observability,

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:18.720
which is probably the biggest
and the most important thing

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:20.303
when it comes to
distributed systems--

00:10:20.303 --> 00:10:22.200
What is happening
in my environment?

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:24.567
Previously, everything was
kind of hidden in the logs.

00:10:24.567 --> 00:10:26.400
I could just look at
the logs in a monolith,

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:28.650
and I could see this
function has this error,

00:10:28.650 --> 00:10:30.370
and this function
is not working.

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:31.170
But now I have--

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:32.520
Is the problem in the network?

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:33.720
Is the problem in a request?

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:34.860
Is the problem in the API?

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:36.460
Is the problem with security?

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:38.100
So I need to measure
a lot of things--

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:40.140
things like metrics,
network metrics,

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:41.727
things like distributed tracing.

00:10:41.727 --> 00:10:44.310
Most of the time you're not just
talking about two containers.

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:46.227
You may be talking about
hundreds or thousands

00:10:46.227 --> 00:10:46.830
of containers.

00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:48.390
And a single
request can traverse

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:51.588
a lot of different services and
a lot of different containers.

00:10:51.588 --> 00:10:53.130
And things like
topology, I just want

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:54.690
to see what my
topology looks like.

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:58.167
Which services are actually
talking to other services?

00:10:58.167 --> 00:11:00.250
Now, if you put a box
around all of these features

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:01.990
that we need in the
middle, they all

00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:05.320
exist, because you have
decided to take a monolith

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:07.790
and put those
things on a network.

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:09.350
These are not your
business logic.

00:11:09.350 --> 00:11:12.380
These are not the things
that make you money.

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:14.767
And these are very network
functions oriented things.

00:11:14.767 --> 00:11:16.350
This is something
you absolutely need.

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:17.892
You cannot live
without these things.

00:11:17.892 --> 00:11:19.940
You have to have them somewhere.

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:21.740
What a service
mesh proposes is--

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:23.690
Why don't instead of
putting all of this logic

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:26.480
into every single container
and as part of libraries,

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:28.730
is there a way to separate
your business logic away

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:30.140
from those network functions?

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:31.610
And what that visually
would look like is

00:11:31.610 --> 00:11:33.027
I want to take
this picture, and I

00:11:33.027 --> 00:11:34.517
want to convert that into this.

00:11:34.517 --> 00:11:36.350
I have my business logic
now abstracted away

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:37.820
from all of my
network functions--

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:40.565
my identity, my observability,
my policies, my authentication

00:11:40.565 --> 00:11:42.025
and authorization.

00:11:42.025 --> 00:11:43.650
Since we live in a
world of containers,

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:46.250
we can toss both of those
things in the container.

00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:49.020
And in Kubernetes, we can put
multiple containers inside

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:50.137
of a Pod.

00:11:50.137 --> 00:11:51.720
Now, this is called
a sidecar pattern,

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:53.345
where you have your
primary container--

00:11:53.345 --> 00:11:55.590
that's your application
container that's running up--

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:57.540
and then you have
a sidecar container

00:11:57.540 --> 00:11:59.370
that's providing
additional functionality

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:01.090
to that primary container.

00:12:01.090 --> 00:12:02.760
And that sidecar
container suspiciously

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:04.843
looks like a network proxy,
because it's providing

00:12:04.843 --> 00:12:06.310
a lot of network functions.

00:12:06.310 --> 00:12:08.070
Envoy is a very
popular proxy that's

00:12:08.070 --> 00:12:09.278
used in distributed system.

00:12:09.278 --> 00:12:11.070
That's used for
client-side load balancing,

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:13.620
because now I'm going
to slap this Envoy proxy

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:15.810
to every single container.

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:18.020
There are a lot of operational
benefits to this too.

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:20.760
I have a very clear
lines of delineation

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:23.550
between roles and responsibility
on who's doing what.

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:25.950
Above this blue line
is my business logic

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:27.850
or my application developers.

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:30.820
They can just now focus on
developing the application.

00:12:30.820 --> 00:12:32.640
Below this line
are my operators.

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:34.715
They can focus on
building the platform,

00:12:34.715 --> 00:12:36.090
putting policies
on the platform,

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:37.950
and enforcing those policies.

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:40.560
This is to me the true
definition of portability.

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:42.840
This container now can
run anywhere as long

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:45.390
as those functions is
provided by the platform.

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:48.600
And now I've relegated these
additional network functions

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:51.563
onto the platform layer that
are managed by experts that

00:12:51.563 --> 00:12:53.730
know how to manage networks,
how to manage security,

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:56.390
how to manage observability.

00:12:56.390 --> 00:12:58.350
So let's look at an
implementation of this.

00:12:58.350 --> 00:12:59.870
How do we implement this thing?

00:12:59.870 --> 00:13:02.090
Istio and Anthos
Service Mesh are just

00:13:02.090 --> 00:13:03.470
two of the implementation.

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:05.120
Istio is an open
source implementation

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:06.860
of a service mesh.

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:09.800
Anthos is a Google
managed service mesh

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:11.852
that uses the same APIs,
uses the Istio APIs.

00:13:11.852 --> 00:13:14.060
And I'm going to build the
control plane and the data

00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:15.090
plane at the same time.

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:16.730
And I'm really just going to
focus on the functionality,

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:18.480
because I think that's
the important bits.

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:21.155
And the implementation
is not as exciting.

00:13:21.155 --> 00:13:23.030
So on the data plane,
we have a bunch of Pods

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:25.200
with the Envoy proxies
running on each Pod.

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:26.120
And then we have
this control plane.

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:27.650
And the first thing
we need to know

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:30.537
is that Istio and ASM needs
their own service registry,

00:13:30.537 --> 00:13:32.120
which is nothing
more than just a list

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:34.830
of all the services that are
running in the service mesh

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:36.830
and all of the endpoints
on how I can get to it.

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:39.260
So these are the IP addresses
of each of the Pods.

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:42.610
These could also be the IP
addresses of each of the VMs.

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:44.760
And it doesn't matter where
these Pods are running.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:46.468
They could be running
on a single cluster

00:13:46.468 --> 00:13:47.510
or multiple clusters.

00:13:47.510 --> 00:13:50.930
With this, I get additional
benefits of traffic management.

00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:52.440
I can do very granular things.

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:55.550
For example, if I'm deploying
a new version of service B

00:13:55.550 --> 00:13:57.778
or Pod B, which is Pod
B prime, and I only

00:13:57.778 --> 00:13:59.570
want to send a subset
of the traffic there,

00:13:59.570 --> 00:14:02.270
now I can do things like I just
want to send 10% of the traffic

00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:04.280
to the new version and
90% of the traffic that

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:05.720
remains to my old version.

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:07.190
I can test out the new version.

00:14:07.190 --> 00:14:08.130
Everything looks good.

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:11.660
And then I can slowly
ramp that new version up.

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:14.150
Observability, where previously
we talked about logging

00:14:14.150 --> 00:14:16.490
was the only thing in the
monolith that was important.

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:19.370
And now we need network
observability, network metrics,

00:14:19.370 --> 00:14:20.540
distributed tracing.

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:23.960
Because these Envoys are placed
in a very fortunate position,

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:26.180
they wrap up all
of these metrics.

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:28.340
And they emit those
in Prometheus specs.

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:30.660
And then you can send them
to any collector you'd like.

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:33.795
So with Istio and Anthos Service
Mesh and GC, automatically

00:14:33.795 --> 00:14:36.170
we ingest them into cloud
monitoring, which is previously

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:37.220
known as Stackdriver.

00:14:37.220 --> 00:14:39.560
But you can use any
collector you'd like.

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:42.107
Security was another thing
that we wanted to talk about.

00:14:42.107 --> 00:14:43.190
It's a zero trust network.

00:14:43.190 --> 00:14:44.550
We don't trust the intranet.

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:46.490
We want everything
to be encrypted.

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:49.147
These Envoys now are kind of
the holders of your identity.

00:14:49.147 --> 00:14:50.480
And they hold a strong identity.

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:51.550
They hold certificates.

00:14:51.550 --> 00:14:54.350
A certificate authority
with a shared common root

00:14:54.350 --> 00:14:57.087
can now furnish the certificates
to each of these Envoys.

00:14:57.087 --> 00:14:58.670
And on the basis of
that, they can now

00:14:58.670 --> 00:15:00.545
authenticate to each
other, and they can also

00:15:00.545 --> 00:15:03.690
do TLS and encryption
between each other.

00:15:03.690 --> 00:15:05.340
Policy, not only
just network policy.

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:08.250
We talked about network
policy as, for example, Pod A

00:15:08.250 --> 00:15:10.680
wants to talk to Pod
C, and maybe we've

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:13.770
limited that to only do
100 requests per second.

00:15:13.770 --> 00:15:16.740
But authorization, role-based
access authorization too,

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:17.490
which is--

00:15:17.490 --> 00:15:20.160
What can actually
Pod A do to Pod C.

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:24.505
So it can do a GET and a POST on
these particular API endpoints.

00:15:24.505 --> 00:15:26.880
That can also be done through
Istio and through the Envoy

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:28.080
proxies.

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:29.820
And last but not
least is the platform

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:31.107
and the service resiliency.

00:15:31.107 --> 00:15:32.940
A circuit breaker is a
common pattern that's

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:35.780
used in production per service.

00:15:35.780 --> 00:15:38.430
So this is this idea that if
a service is having an issue,

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:41.550
we want to be able to fill that
service as quickly as possible

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:43.210
and take countermeasures
against it.

00:15:43.210 --> 00:15:46.830
So let's say we have a flow that
goes from Pod A to B to C to D,

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:48.660
and D is having problems.

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:50.280
Instead of that
flow just continuing

00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:54.150
to run and eventually just cause
backwards cascading failures,

00:15:54.150 --> 00:15:57.720
we want D to quickly identify
that I'm having some issues.

00:15:57.720 --> 00:15:59.220
And the other
services can then just

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:01.900
take countermeasures and
precautions on top of that.

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:03.420
So in this case,
let's say we put

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:06.750
a rule that says, "If I see
five consecutive HTTP errors,

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:07.800
just consider me down.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:09.300
Go around me, and
do something else.

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:10.530
Notify the user.

00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:13.080
Tell them to wait or back
off or any of these logics

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:14.500
that you can put in."

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:16.000
And fault injection
is something you

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:18.470
can do to test the resiliency
of your application.

00:16:18.470 --> 00:16:22.000
So how would my application work
if I injected a 10% error rate,

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:24.490
or if I injected a
10 millisecond delay?

00:16:24.490 --> 00:16:27.293
This is also something one of my
customers used to actually test

00:16:27.293 --> 00:16:28.960
something where they
wanted to deploy it

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:31.800
into a different region that
was about 50 milliseconds away.

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:33.925
And instead of spinning up
an entire infrastructure

00:16:33.925 --> 00:16:37.270
in that region, they just simply
injected a 50 millisecond delay

00:16:37.270 --> 00:16:38.650
and just tested
and observed what

00:16:38.650 --> 00:16:40.790
their services are going to do.

00:16:40.790 --> 00:16:42.775
So these are the six
features and functionality

00:16:42.775 --> 00:16:44.900
of the control plane that
are above and beyond what

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:45.890
Kubernetes gives you.

00:16:45.890 --> 00:16:48.440
Service registry works
across multiple clusters.

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:51.740
Traffic management can happen
at a very granular service

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:53.110
per service level.

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:54.710
Observability gives
you extra things

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:56.377
without having to
instrument your thing.

00:16:56.377 --> 00:16:58.790
You get security and
policy above and beyond

00:16:58.790 --> 00:17:00.200
what Kubernetes gives you.

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:02.230
And then you get resiliency
on when Kubernetes

00:17:02.230 --> 00:17:04.430
is having problems and
services are having problems,

00:17:04.430 --> 00:17:09.420
how can I automatically recover
from those particular services?

00:17:09.420 --> 00:17:10.130
So that's great.

00:17:10.130 --> 00:17:12.140
So we have this distributed
services concept.

00:17:12.140 --> 00:17:13.897
We have this concept
of a service mesh.

00:17:13.897 --> 00:17:15.230
Let's build a platform together.

00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:16.730
And let's build that on GCP.

00:17:16.730 --> 00:17:18.188
So the first thing
I'm going to say

00:17:18.188 --> 00:17:19.940
is that a lot of these
concepts can apply

00:17:19.940 --> 00:17:21.107
on non-GCP environments too.

00:17:21.107 --> 00:17:23.023
And we're only going to
build one environment.

00:17:23.023 --> 00:17:25.640
So think of this as this is
my production environment,

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:29.540
and I'm about to onboard
a service or a product

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:30.500
in multiple regions.

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:32.833
And we're going to just pick
two regions, because if you

00:17:32.833 --> 00:17:35.967
can do this in two regions, you
can do this in 20-plus regions.

00:17:35.967 --> 00:17:37.550
So everything starts
with the network.

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:38.480
And what we want
to do is we want

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:40.040
to have a nice flat network.

00:17:40.040 --> 00:17:42.290
It makes communication
between Pods

00:17:42.290 --> 00:17:45.110
and between Pods and other
services much, much easier.

00:17:45.110 --> 00:17:47.670
And we want to wrap that
around into a single network.

00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:50.180
In Google, we have a concept
of a shared VPC that can

00:17:50.180 --> 00:17:52.650
be shared to other projects.

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:54.590
So we have this one
project, project 0, that's

00:17:54.590 --> 00:17:56.400
owned by your networking team.

00:17:56.400 --> 00:17:58.045
And it gives them
centralized control.

00:17:58.045 --> 00:18:00.420
So instead of giving every
single team their own network,

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:02.450
you have this one
centralized network.

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:04.100
They control the
ingress and egress

00:18:04.100 --> 00:18:05.430
out of that particular network.

00:18:05.430 --> 00:18:07.160
And it makes it much
easier for services

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:09.590
to communicate with one another.

00:18:09.590 --> 00:18:11.500
Inside of that,
we have projects.

00:18:11.500 --> 00:18:13.280
Now project in Google
is just a collection

00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:16.560
of resources owned by a certain
team or by a certain function.

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:18.810
So in this case, we're just
representing two projects.

00:18:18.810 --> 00:18:20.435
We have a platform_admin
project that's

00:18:20.435 --> 00:18:22.143
owned by the platform
admins team, that's

00:18:22.143 --> 00:18:23.060
your operator team.

00:18:23.060 --> 00:18:25.730
And then we have this
product1 project that's

00:18:25.730 --> 00:18:27.170
owned by a single product team.

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:28.920
And imagine if you
have multiple products,

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:32.005
then you would have project
3, project 4, and so on.

00:18:32.005 --> 00:18:33.380
When I think of
projects, I think

00:18:33.380 --> 00:18:35.870
of three levels of controls.

00:18:35.870 --> 00:18:37.730
Billing is done at
a project level.

00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:40.010
So anytime I want
separation of billing-- so

00:18:40.010 --> 00:18:42.110
by environment, by
team, by function--

00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:44.720
it's a good way to put a
project boundary around it.

00:18:44.720 --> 00:18:47.408
Access management is
done at a project level.

00:18:47.408 --> 00:18:48.950
So things that are
owned by let's say

00:18:48.950 --> 00:18:51.720
the platform admins team should
belong into the platform admins

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:52.220
project.

00:18:52.220 --> 00:18:54.095
And things that are
owned by the product team

00:18:54.095 --> 00:18:55.600
should belong to the product.

00:18:55.600 --> 00:18:58.163
And last but not least, quota
is done at a project level.

00:18:58.163 --> 00:19:00.830
So where you don't want to share
quotas between different teams,

00:19:00.830 --> 00:19:03.843
it's much easier to just put
a project boundary around it.

00:19:03.843 --> 00:19:05.760
We also put environments
in separate projects.

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:07.632
So here I'm showing
a three-project setup

00:19:07.632 --> 00:19:09.090
for let's say the
prod environment.

00:19:09.090 --> 00:19:11.298
You would have a very similar
setup for dev and stage

00:19:11.298 --> 00:19:13.290
as well.

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:15.330
Then inside of these
projects, we put subnets.

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:18.270
Subnets in GCP and
Google are regional.

00:19:18.270 --> 00:19:21.030
And we recommend you use
as lowest number of subnets

00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:22.150
as possible.

00:19:22.150 --> 00:19:23.880
So here I'm representing
two regions.

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:25.200
On the left-hand side,
we have region 1.

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:26.860
On the right-hand
side, we have region 2.

00:19:26.860 --> 00:19:27.902
And we have two subnets--

00:19:27.902 --> 00:19:28.860
subnet 1 and subnet 2.

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:29.943
You can have more subnets.

00:19:29.943 --> 00:19:32.250
But it's much easier to
expand a single subnet

00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:35.160
than to have smaller subnets
where you didn't maybe

00:19:35.160 --> 00:19:37.690
size them right the first way.

00:19:37.690 --> 00:19:39.940
IPAM, IP address management,
becomes much easier.

00:19:39.940 --> 00:19:41.040
You have to start
thinking about--

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:42.510
How many IPs do
I need for nodes?

00:19:42.510 --> 00:19:44.010
How many IPs do
I need for Pods--

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:46.800
which are the biggest
resource hogs for IPs--

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:47.992
and for service IPs?

00:19:47.992 --> 00:19:49.700
We always recommend
using custom subnets,

00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:54.822
so not having IP wastage, by
having these automated subnets.

00:19:54.822 --> 00:19:57.280
Inside of those subnets, you
have your Kubernetes clusters.

00:19:57.280 --> 00:19:59.250
Now these are your
Kubernetes clusters

00:19:59.250 --> 00:20:02.165
that are there for applications
as well as for operations.

00:20:02.165 --> 00:20:04.290
So in this case, we're
showing a six-cluster setup.

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:06.015
And they're both identical.

00:20:06.015 --> 00:20:08.640
There's three clusters in region
1, three clusters in region 2.

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:10.170
If you want other
regions, you can

00:20:10.170 --> 00:20:12.780
think about horizontally
scaling this out and basically

00:20:12.780 --> 00:20:16.350
stamping this exact environment
in other regions as well.

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:19.000
We have these colorful
clusters-- the orange, red,

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:19.830
blue, and green.

00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:21.340
Think of them as your
application clusters.

00:20:21.340 --> 00:20:22.650
This is where your
actual services

00:20:22.650 --> 00:20:25.050
that are used by other services
or used by your customers

00:20:25.050 --> 00:20:26.133
are actually going to sit.

00:20:26.133 --> 00:20:28.492
We'll call them apps clusters,
or application clusters.

00:20:28.492 --> 00:20:30.450
Then you have these two
clusters at the bottom,

00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:31.860
the gray clusters.

00:20:31.860 --> 00:20:33.863
Think of them as your
operations clusters.

00:20:33.863 --> 00:20:36.030
This is where your shared
services are going to sit.

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:39.420
This is things like CI/CD,
tooling, observability,

00:20:39.420 --> 00:20:43.460
or, in this case, the ASM
or the Istio control plane.

00:20:43.460 --> 00:20:45.263
By separating out your
applications cluster

00:20:45.263 --> 00:20:46.680
and your operations
cluster, we've

00:20:46.680 --> 00:20:48.690
kind of separated out
the lifecycle of the two.

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:51.270
We want to manage those
independently of one another.

00:20:51.270 --> 00:20:54.960
Your applications probably will
have a much higher scrutiny

00:20:54.960 --> 00:20:56.430
for SLOs and stuff.

00:20:56.430 --> 00:20:58.350
And maybe they're not
being upgraded as often,

00:20:58.350 --> 00:21:00.600
and we want to maybe
really take care of those.

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:02.828
As opposed to your
operations clusters,

00:21:02.828 --> 00:21:04.870
which can go down for an
extended period of time.

00:21:04.870 --> 00:21:07.240
And it may or should not
affect too many things.

00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:09.150
So by separating those
two clusters out,

00:21:09.150 --> 00:21:11.640
I have granular control
over how I upgrade

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:15.390
and how I maintain and
operate on these clusters.

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:20.095
We started with a single ASM
control plane per region.

00:21:20.095 --> 00:21:22.220
We can put a control plane
in every single cluster.

00:21:22.220 --> 00:21:23.760
That's a perfectly
viable design.

00:21:23.760 --> 00:21:25.040
And if you have a small
number of clusters,

00:21:25.040 --> 00:21:27.200
as a matter of fact, that
is what's recommended.

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:29.810
But then we realized that as
you grow-- as you go beyond

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:32.235
let's say 20, 30,
40, or 100 clusters--

00:21:32.235 --> 00:21:33.860
then you would have
100 control planes,

00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:36.152
and it becomes an operational
challenge and a nightmare

00:21:36.152 --> 00:21:37.860
to manage those.

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:40.940
We also do a single
control plane per region.

00:21:40.940 --> 00:21:43.610
This way you're not
dependent upon other regions

00:21:43.610 --> 00:21:45.830
or regional connectivity
or even latency.

00:21:45.830 --> 00:21:47.960
Within a region, your
latency is very, very small.

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:49.335
We put the control
plane in there

00:21:49.335 --> 00:21:50.910
in the ops cluster
at the bottom.

00:21:50.910 --> 00:21:53.420
And now you can just continue
to add application clusters.

00:21:53.420 --> 00:21:55.805
And they all connect back to
that single control plane.

00:21:55.805 --> 00:21:57.680
And we've already talked
about the separation

00:21:57.680 --> 00:21:59.555
of lifecycle management
as well as ownership,

00:21:59.555 --> 00:22:02.570
where your application clusters
and operation clusters could

00:22:02.570 --> 00:22:04.470
be owned by different teams.

00:22:04.470 --> 00:22:07.570
So now you have even though
it's logically a global mesh,

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:11.180
but you have these
regional meshes

00:22:11.180 --> 00:22:13.730
that are in a shared
control plane module.

00:22:13.730 --> 00:22:15.290
So you have a
single control plane

00:22:15.290 --> 00:22:18.380
sharing all of the clusters
within that region.

00:22:18.380 --> 00:22:21.500
But both of these control
planes have the full picture

00:22:21.500 --> 00:22:27.480
of the entire infrastructure,
of every single cluster.

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:28.720
Then comes in namespaces.

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:30.780
Namespaces is a very
important concept when

00:22:30.780 --> 00:22:32.310
it comes to service meshes.

00:22:32.310 --> 00:22:35.010
In a globally
scalable service mesh,

00:22:35.010 --> 00:22:37.620
in a single logical
global service mesh,

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:39.330
a namespace across
multiple clusters

00:22:39.330 --> 00:22:41.065
is assumed to be the same thing.

00:22:41.065 --> 00:22:42.690
So in this particular
case, if I create

00:22:42.690 --> 00:22:44.910
a namespace called
product1-namespace

00:22:44.910 --> 00:22:47.790
on four different clusters,
and I have workloads running

00:22:47.790 --> 00:22:50.100
in these namespaces,
from the Istio service

00:22:50.100 --> 00:22:52.350
registry or the Anthos Service
Mesh service registry's

00:22:52.350 --> 00:22:55.863
perspective, it's considered
a single distributed service.

00:22:55.863 --> 00:22:57.780
Aha, got that word back--
distributed service.

00:22:57.780 --> 00:22:59.220
We're starting to
talk about that.

00:22:59.220 --> 00:23:01.290
I'm getting regional
redundancy, because I

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:03.500
have multiple clusters
within that region.

00:23:03.500 --> 00:23:05.250
I'm getting global
redundancy, because I'm

00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:08.640
running the same service now
across multiple clusters.

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:10.380
I'm also utilizing
my clusters better,

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:12.720
because now I can create
namespaces as these landing

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:14.323
zones for my services.

00:23:14.323 --> 00:23:15.990
I don't have to create
separate clusters

00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:18.060
for individual services.

00:23:18.060 --> 00:23:21.658
Namespaces are the landing zones
for, in this case, product 1.

00:23:21.658 --> 00:23:23.700
And that's what I'm renting
out or I'm giving out

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:24.825
to that particular service.

00:23:24.825 --> 00:23:27.030
If I have product 2,
product 3, product N,

00:23:27.030 --> 00:23:28.560
those will all be
namespaces that

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:30.570
are placed on these
clusters, all part

00:23:30.570 --> 00:23:32.570
of the single
global service mesh.

00:23:32.570 --> 00:23:35.200
And service meshes can
talk to each other,

00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:37.980
whether it's running in one
cluster or multiple clusters.

00:23:37.980 --> 00:23:40.230
And since all of these
clusters are on a flat network,

00:23:40.230 --> 00:23:41.640
I never leave my VPC.

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:45.860
Everything is done on the
same virtual private cloud.

00:23:45.860 --> 00:23:47.900
So think of namespaces
and landing zones.

00:23:47.900 --> 00:23:50.180
And think of multi-tenant
clusters always.

00:23:50.180 --> 00:23:52.910
Multi-tenant clusters give
you the best resiliency.

00:23:52.910 --> 00:23:56.150
So even in this example,
I've got a product 1 service

00:23:56.150 --> 00:23:57.260
running on four clusters.

00:23:57.260 --> 00:24:00.110
Technically, again, I can lose
three of these four clusters,

00:24:00.110 --> 00:24:02.160
and I've never lost my service.

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:05.000
So I lose an entire region
and part of my other region,

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:06.500
and I have not lost it.

00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:08.790
Versus if I had
individual clusters

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:12.230
that I'm not amortizing
between different services.

00:24:12.230 --> 00:24:15.770
Now these product 1 services
can talk to other GCP resources

00:24:15.770 --> 00:24:21.220
that are running in project 2
in that particular product team.

00:24:21.220 --> 00:24:23.500
So this is a good way
to think about how

00:24:23.500 --> 00:24:25.630
to manage multiple clusters
in multiple regions,

00:24:25.630 --> 00:24:27.970
how to overlay a
service mesh on top,

00:24:27.970 --> 00:24:29.560
how to think about
project hierarchy

00:24:29.560 --> 00:24:33.280
as well as networking, how we
think about IAM and controls.

00:24:33.280 --> 00:24:36.010
This, what we see, is a good
way to think about globally

00:24:36.010 --> 00:24:36.800
scalable services.

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:38.925
And, by the way, this is
how Google builds services

00:24:38.925 --> 00:24:39.802
internally.

00:24:39.802 --> 00:24:42.260
If you want to learn more, and
if you want to build exactly

00:24:42.260 --> 00:24:44.302
what I showed you, you
can go to the first link--

00:24:44.302 --> 00:24:46.340
bit.ly/asm-workshop.

00:24:46.340 --> 00:24:48.490
That will give you
actual terraform scripts

00:24:48.490 --> 00:24:50.750
to build exactly
what we showed--

00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:53.630
as well as you can learn about
how to deploy applications,

00:24:53.630 --> 00:24:56.490
how to secure applications,
how to observe them, and so on.

00:24:56.490 --> 00:24:58.430
And if you want to learn more
about multi-cluster lifecycle

00:24:58.430 --> 00:24:58.940
management--

00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:01.790
Well, now I'm going into six
or seven or eight clusters.

00:25:01.790 --> 00:25:03.290
How do I upgrade those clusters?

00:25:03.290 --> 00:25:05.660
And how do I manage and
operate multiple clusters?

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:07.670
You can learn more on
the blog series I wrote.

00:25:07.670 --> 00:25:09.860
It's a five-part blog
series on the second link,

00:25:09.860 --> 00:25:13.640
which is bit.ly/gke
multicluster lifecycle.

00:25:13.640 --> 00:25:15.350
That shows you how to
upgrade one cluster

00:25:15.350 --> 00:25:17.540
at a time and different
strategies of managing,

00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:21.450
monitoring, and maintaining your
multiple cluster environment.

00:25:21.450 --> 00:25:23.260
Thank you very much.

